Dear Judith,

Thursday morning works for me. We can compare all 32 transcriptions with the =
paper copies then. I have attached v0.2 of the seven passages with my comment=
s on your provisional translations; I have left the French and your wording i=
n place for us to check together.

Could you have 085 and 186 ready to open at once, including the sentences on =
either side? In 085 we need to read beyond the words about the heating gift; =
in 186 the sentence before the instruction to collect matters for the distinc=
tion between reading in the room and taking a book out. I would like to check=
 both against the paper before settling the English or the implications for e=
ntry.

Best,
Julie

On 17 October 2024 at 16:08, Judith Morris wrote:
> Could we go through the transcriptions of all 32 letters against the paper =
copies on Thursday morning, 24 October? I have attached the seven passages wh=
ere I am least confident of my English. I have left my first wording in the d=
ocument so that we can see what needs to change.
>
> For each, could we also follow who is asking whom for money, and who is to =
receive it? I am particularly unsure about 085 and 186: either reading may ch=
ange what I have said about conditions in Chapter 2. It would help to have th=
e sentences on either side of those passages open as well.

